    "subject": "Chemistry",
    "chapter": "Chemical Reactions",
    "topic": "Equilibrium",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class=\"container-fluid\">\r\n\t<div class=\"row align-items-start\">\r\n\t\t<div class=\"col-8\">\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>A reaction which can go in forward and backward direction simultaneously is called reversible reaction.</li>\r\n<li>In a system at equilibrium, both orward and reverse reactions are running simultaneously. The conversion between reactants and products is depicted by a double arrow.</li>\r\n\\[CaCO_3 \\rightleftharpoons CO_2  + CaO \\]\r\n<li>In chemical equilibrium the concentrations of reactants and products remain constant over time.</li>\r\n<li>At equilibrium the forward and reverse reactions are proceeding at the same rate.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\nEquilibrium Constant of a Chemical Reaction:\r\n\\[aA + bB \\rightleftharpoons cC + dD\\]\r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$K_{EQ} = \\dfrac{[C]^c[D]^d}{[A]^a[B]^b}$ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 85</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n\r\n\t\t</div>\r\n    \t\t<div class=\"col\">\r\n     <center><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/ChemicalEquilibrium.svg\" alt=\"Chemical Equilibrium\" width=\"340\"><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ChemicalEquilibrium.svg\">Fintelia</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n    \t\t</div>\r\n  \t</div>\r\n</div>",
