    "discipline": "Other",
    "subject": "Electricity and Magnetism",
    "chapter": "AC Circuits",
    "topic": "Frequency and Period of AC Waveform",
    "subtopic": "DC vs AC",
    "content_html": "<div class=\"notes-image-container my-4 text-center flex flex-col items-center\"><center><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8b/Alternating-current-diagram.svg\" style=\"width:340px;\"><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alternating-current-diagram.svg\">MikeRun</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via Wikimedia Commons<br/></small></small></center></div>In DC, voltage remains constant over time, whereas in AC it changes sinusoidally as shown in the following waveform.\r\n<p>Voltage in an AC circuit is given by: \r\n\r\n\\[V = V_{max}\\ \\sin \\omega t\\]\r\n\r\nFor a sinusoidal voltage or current of frequency f (Hz) and period T (seconds),\r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$f = \\dfrac{1}{T} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi}$ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 361</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n\r\nwhere $\\omega$ = angular frequency in radians/s",
    "important": "",
    "resources": "<div class=\"mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 flex flex-wrap gap-2\"><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jyiI_hGQVW1YOLAFOfqazQMsyofx95rx/preview\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"px-3 py-1.5 bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-900/40 text-purple-700 dark:text-purple-300 rounded text-xs font-bold hover:bg-purple-200 transition-colors\">👨‍🏫 Tutoring</a></div>",
