    "discipline": "Other",
    "subject": "Engineering Economics",
    "chapter": "Economic Analyses",
    "topic": "Payback Period Analyses",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<p><strong>The payback period</strong> (PP) is the number of years for the projects to break even, i.e. the number of years for which discounted annual net cash flows must be summed before the sum becomes positive (and remains positive for the remainder of the projects life).<br />\r\nThe length of time required to recover the cost of an investment. The payback period of a given investment or project is an important determinant of whether to undertake the position or project, as longer payback periods are typically not desirable for investment positions.<br />\r\nCalculated as: <span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathrm{Payback\\ Period} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{Cost\\ of \\ Project}}{\\mathrm{Annual\\ Cash\\ Inflows}}\\]</span> Payback period, though is easy to understand and calculate, may not give you accurate results as it does not consider time value of money. Also, it gives you a time value but it does not tell you what happens after initial investment is recovered.</p>",
    "important": "\\[\\mathrm{Payback\\ Period} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{Cost\\ of \\ Project}}{\\mathrm{Annual\\ Cash\\ Inflows}}\\]",
