    "discipline": "Other",
    "subject": "Engineering Economics",
    "chapter": "Non-Annual Compounding",
    "topic": "Non-Annual Compounding",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<p>Non-annual compounding refers to the practice of compounding interest or investment returns at intervals other than once per year. Non-annual compounding is a critical concept in finance, as it affects the growth of investments and the cost of borrowing. Understanding how compounding intervals work and how to calculate future values with non-annual compounding is essential for financial planning, investment decisions.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Compounding is the process of earning interest or returns on an investment, not only on the initial principal amount but also on any interest or returns that have already been earned. In most financial scenarios, the interest or returns are compounded annually, meaning they are added to the principal once a year. However, there are situations where compounding occurs more frequently, such as semi-annually, quarterly, monthly, or even daily.</p>\r\n\r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$i_e = \\left(1 + \\dfrac{r}{m}\\right)^m - 1$ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 231</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>",
    "important": "\\[i_e = \\left(1 + \\dfrac{r}{m}\\right)^m - 1\\]",
    "resources": "",
    "images": [],
    "local_images": [],
    "video": "assets/videos/Nominal_vs_Effective_Annual_Interest_Rate.mp4",
    "videoTitle": "Nominal vs. Effective Annual Interest Rate"
