    "chapter": "Fluid Properties",
    "topic": "Stress, Pressure and Viscosity",
    "subtopic": "Newtonian vs Non-Newtonian Fluids",
    "content_html": "<h5><span><b>Newtonian vs. Non-Newtonian Fluids:</b></span></h5>\n<div class=\"container-fluid\">\n  <div class=\"row align-items-start\">\n    <div class=\"col-lg-8\">\n      <p>Fluids are classified based on the relationship between applied shear stress ($\\tau$) and the resulting rate of shear deformation ($du/dy$):</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Newtonian Fluids:</strong> Exhibit a linear relationship between shear stress and rate of shear deformation. The dynamic viscosity ($\\mu$) remains constant regardless of shear rate:\n          \\[\\tau = \\mu \\dfrac{du}{dy}\\]\n          <em>Examples:</em> Water, air, gasoline, light oils, alcohol.\n        </li>\n        <li><strong>Non-Newtonian Fluids:</strong> Viscosity changes with shear rate or time:\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>Pseudoplastic (Shear-Thinning):</strong> Viscosity decreases with increasing shear rate (e.g., paint, blood, polymer solutions).</li>\n            <li><strong>Dilatant (Shear-Thickening):</strong> Viscosity increases with increasing shear rate (e.g., cornstarch in water/oobleck, quicksand).</li>\n            <li><strong>Bingham Plastics:</strong> Require a minimum yield stress $\\tau_y$ before flow begins (e.g., toothpaste, ketchup, drilling mud, mayonnaise):\n              \\[\\tau = \\tau_y + \\mu_p \\dfrac{du}{dy}\\]\n            </li>\n          </ul>\n        </li>\n      </ul>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"col-lg-4 text-center\">\n      <center><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/44/Viscosities.gif\" alt=\"Viscosities\" width=\"340\"><br/>\n      <small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Viscosities.gif\">Synapticrelay</a>, CC BY-SA 4.0</small></small></center>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>",
    "important": "Newton's Law of Viscosity:\r\n\\[\\tau = \\mu \\dfrac{du}{dy}\\]\r\nBingham Plastic:\r\n\\[\\tau = \\tau_y + \\mu_p \\dfrac{du}{dy}\\]",
