    "discipline": "Other",
    "subject": "Fluid Mechanics",
    "chapter": "Fluid Statics",
    "topic": "Hydrostatic Forces on Submerged Surfaces",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class=\"container-fluid\">\r\n\t<div class=\"row align-items-start\">\r\n\t\t<div class=\"col col-lg-5\">\r\n<p><strong>Center of Pressure</strong> is defined as the point of application of the resultant pressure. The depth of center of pressure of an immersed surface from free surface of the liquid is given by:</p>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n\t<li>\r\n<p>For vertically immersed surface, \r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$y_{CP} = y_C + \\dfrac{I_{xc}}{y_C A}$ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 180</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n\r\ny$_C$ = slant distance from liquid surface to the centroid of area<br />\r\ny$_C$= <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\dfrac{h_C}{\\sin \\theta}\\)</span><br />\r\nh$_C$ = vertical distance from liquid surface to centroid of area<br />\r\ny$_{CP}$ = slant distance from liquid surface to center of pressure<br />\r\n$\\theta$ = angle between liquid surface and edge of submerged surface<br />\r\nI$_{xc}$</span> = moment of inertia about the centroidal x-axis</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p>This equation indicates that the centre of the pressure is always below the centroid of the submerged plane.</p>\r\n\r\n\t\t</div>\r\n    \t\t<div class=\"col\">\r\n     <center><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Hydrostatic_force_on_submerged_plane.png\" alt=\"Hydrostatic force on submerged plane\" width=\"600\"><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hydrostatic_force_on_submerged_plane.png\">Ertunc</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n    \t\t</div>\r\n  \t</div>\r\n</div>",
    "important": "\\[y_{CP} = y_C + \\dfrac{I_{xC}}{y_C A}\\]",
    "resources": "",
