    "discipline": "Other",
    "subject": "Material Properties and Processing",
    "chapter": "Phase Diagrams/Heat Treating",
    "topic": "Important Points in Iron-Iron Carbide Phase Diagram",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class=\"container-fluid\">\r\n\t<div class=\"row align-items-start\">\r\n\t\t<div class=\"col col-lg-6\">\r\n\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n\t<li>\r\nEutectic: Eutectic Point refers to the point in a phase diagram indicating the chemical composition and temperature corresponding to the lowest melting point of a mixture of components. In case of Iron-Iron-carbide diagram, eutectic is at 4.30 wt%C, 1147 deg C.\r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">Eutectic reaction (liquid $\\rightarrow$  two solid phases)<span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 128</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n\r\n\t</li>\r\n\t<li>\r\nEutectoid: A eutectoid reaction is a three-phase reaction by which, on cooling, a solid transforms into two other solid phases at the same time. If the bottom of a single-phase solid field closes (and provided the adjacent two-phase fields are solid also), it does so with a eutectoid point. In case of Iron-Iron-carbide diagram, eutectoid is at 0.76 wt%C, 727 Deg C. \r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">Eutectoid reaction (solid $\\rightarrow$ two solid phases) <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 128</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n\t</li>\r\n<li>A peritectic reaction is a reaction where a solid phase and liquid phase will together form a second solid phase at a particular temperature and composition.\r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">Peritectic reaction (liquid + solid $\\rightarrow$ solid) <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 128</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n</li>\r\n<li>A peritectoid reaction is the transformation of two solid phases in an alloy system, forming a new phase.\r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">Peritectoid reaction (two solid phases $\\rightarrow$ solid) <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 128</span></button>\r\n</div><br/></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\t\t</div>\r\n    \t\t<div class=\"col\">\r\n     <center><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/Eutectic_system_phase_diagram.svg\" alt=\"Eutectic Point\" width=\"500\"><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eutectic_system_phase_diagram.svg\">Eutektikum_new.svg: *Eutektikum.gif: Dr. Báder Imrederivative work: Michbich (talk)derivative work: Wizard191</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5\">CC BY-SA 2.5</a>, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n    \t\t</div>\r\n  \t</div>\r\n</div>",
