    "discipline": "Other",
    "subject": "Measurements, Instrumentation and Controls",
    "chapter": "Data Acquisition",
    "topic": "Signal Interface",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<h5><span><b>Analog Signal Interfaces:</b></span></h5>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Voltage and Current Signals: Common analog interfaces use voltage (e.g., 0-10V) or current (e.g., 4-20mA) to represent information.</li>\r\n<li>Signal Conditioning: Analog interfaces often require signal conditioning to amplify, filter, or convert signals.</li>\r\n<li>Noise and Interference: Analog signals are susceptible to noise and interference, requiring shielding and filtering measures.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h5><span><b>Digital Signal Interfaces:</b></span></h5>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Digital Logic Levels: Digital interfaces use logic levels (high and low) to represent binary data.</li>\r\n<li>Serial and Parallel Interfaces: Data can be transmitted serially (bit by bit) or in parallel (multiple bits at once).</li>\r\n<li>Common Standards: Examples of digital interface standards include USB, HDMI, SPI, I2C, and Ethernet.</li>\r\n</ul>",
