    "discipline": "Other",
    "subject": "Safety, Health, and Environment",
    "chapter": "Basic Safety Equipment",
    "topic": "Emergency Shutoffs",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class= \"container-fluid\">\r\n  <div class= \"row align-items-start\">\r\n   <div class=\"col col-lg-8\">\r\n   An emergency shutoff is intended:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>To avert arising or to reduce existing hazards to persons, damage to machinery or to work in progress</li>\r\n<li>To be initiated by a single human action when the normal stopping function is inadequate for this purpose.</li>\r\n<li>To completely and as quickly as possible abort the operation, even if it damages the equipment</li>\r\n<li>To be operable in a manner that is quick, simple and be obvious even to untrained operator or bystander.</li>\r\n<li>May feature a removable barrier or other protection against accidental activation (e.g. a plastic cover that must be lifted or glass that must be broken.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h5><span><b>Categories (As per BS EN 418):</b></span></h5>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Category 0: Uncontrolled stop, stopping by immediate removal of power to the machine</li>\r\n<li>Category 1: Controlled stop, with power available to the machine actuators to achieve</li>\r\n<li>Category 2: Controlled stop, with power left available to the machine actuators.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n    </div>\r\n    <div class=\"col\">\r\n<center><p><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c1/Vanne_pour_coupure_d%27urgence_gaz.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 50vh;\"></p><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vanne_pour_coupure_d%27urgence_gaz.jpg\">Cjp24</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center>\r\n    </div>\r\n  </div>\r\n</div>",
