    "discipline": "Other",
    "subject": "Statics",
    "chapter": "Statically Determinate Truss",
    "topic": "Method of Section",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<div class= \"container-fluid\">\r\n  <div class= \"row align-items-start\">\r\n    <div class=\"col col-lg-8\">\r\n   <p>If the forces in only a few members of a truss are to be determined, the method of sections is generally the most appropriate analysis procedure.<br />\r\nThe method of sections consists of passing an imaginary line through the truss, cutting it into sections.<br />\r\nEach imaginary section must be in equilibrium if the entire truss is in equilibrium.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n\t<li>First, if necessary, determine the support reactions for the entire truss.</li>\r\n\t<li>Next, make a decision on how the truss should be cut into sections and draw the corresponding free-body diagrams.</li>\r\n\t<li>Try to apply the three equations of equilibrium such that simultaneous solution is not required.</li>\r\n\t<li>Moments should be summed about points that lie at the intersection of the lines of action of two unknown forces, so that the remaining force may be determined.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n    </div>\r\n    <div class=\"col\">\r\n<center><p><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Truss_Structure_Analysis%2C_Method_of_Sections.png\" style=\"max-width: 50vh;\"></p>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Truss_Structure_Analysis,_Method_of_Sections.png\">Gcd822</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n    </div>\r\n  </div>\r\n</div>",
