    "discipline": "Other",
    "subject": "Thermodynamics and Heat Transfer",
    "chapter": "Properties of Ideal Gases and Pure Substances",
    "topic": "Properties of Steam",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<h5><span><b>Quality of Steam:</b></span></h5>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>The ratio of the mass of the mixture which is vapor (vap) to the total mixture mass is called quality of steam.</p>\r\n\r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$x = \\dfrac{m_g}{(m_g + m_f)}$ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 144</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\nm$_g$ = mass of vapor<br/>\r\nm$_f$ = mass of liquid<br/>\r\n<ul>\r\n\t<li>x = 0, corresponds to $m_{g}$ = 0. This is the all liquid limit.</li>\r\n\t<li>x = 1, corresponds to $m_{f}$ = 0. This is the all gas limit. </li>\r\n\\[0\\leq x\\leq 1\\]\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Saturation pressure</strong> is the pressure at which the liquid and vapor phases are in equilibrium at a given temperature.<br />\r\n<strong>Saturation Temperature</strong> is the temperature at which the liquid and vapor phases are in equilibrium at a given pressure.<br />\r\nInternal Energy, \r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$u = u_f + xu_{fg}$ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 144</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n\r\n\r\nEnthalpy, \r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$h = h_f + xh_{fg}$ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 144</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n\r\n\r\nEntropy, \r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n<button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$s = s_f + xs_{fg}$ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 144</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n\r\n\r\n<h5><span><b>Superheated Steam Properties:</b></span></h5>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n\t<li>Saturated vapor: A term including wet and dry vapor.</li>\r\n\t<li>Dry (saturated) vapor. Vapor which has just completed evaporation. The pressure and temperature of the vapor are the saturation values.</li>\r\n\t<li>Superheated steam: When steam is heated after it has become dry and saturated, it is called superheated steam and the process of heating is called superheating. Superheating is always carried out at constant pressure.</li>\r\n\t<li>Degree of superheat: The term used for the numerical amount by which the temperature of a superheated vapor exceeds the saturation temperature.</li>\r\n</ul>",
