    "discipline": "Other",
    "subject": "Thermodynamics and Heat Transfer",
    "chapter": "Radiation",
    "topic": "Perfectly Black Body",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<center><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d4/Interactions_Between_Light_and_Matter_%2801F8GF7ARTW59HQ4M6MJDA5GE8%29.png\" alt=\"Interaction between light and matter\" style=\"max-width: 80vh;\"><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Interactions_Between_Light_and_Matter_(01F8GF7ARTW59HQ4M6MJDA5GE8).png\">ILLUSTRATION: NASA, ESA, Leah Hustak (STScI)</a>, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n\r\n\r\n<p>A body which absorbs all incident light is referred to ’<strong>Perfectly black body</strong>’. A black body would not reflect and would not transmit any light falling on it.<br />\r\nIn practice, a hollow enclosure having a small hole is the nearest approach to a black body because the radiation entering the chamber through the hole is absorbed completely due to repeated reflections inside the enclosure. Radiation emerging from the small hole of such a hollow enclosure is negligible.<br />\r\n\r\n\r\n<div class= \"container-fluid\">\r\n  <div class= \"row align-items-start\">\r\n   <div class=\"col col-lg-8\">\r\n   <strong>Black body radiation:</strong> Radiation from a black surface depends only on temperature and does not depend on the nature of the surface. At lower temperature radiations are small. With increasing temperature amount of radiations increase but the quantity of radiations with small energy is decreased.</p>\r\n    </div>\r\n    <div class=\"col\">\r\n<center><p><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/32/Black_body.webp\" style=\"max-width: 50vh;\"></p><br/>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_body.webp\">2140474karishma</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/>\r\n    </div>\r\n  </div>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<h5><span><b>Types of Bodies:</b></span></h5>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n\t<li>\r\n\t<p><strong>General Body</strong><br />\r\n\tFor general body,</p>\r\n\r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$\\alpha + \\rho + \\tau = 1$ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 217</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n\r\n\t<p>where<br />\r\n\t<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha\\)</span> = absorptivity (ratio of energy absorbed to incident energy)<br />\r\n\t<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho\\)</span> = reflectivity (ratio of energy reflected to incident energy)<br />\r\n\t<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau\\)</span> = transmissivity (ratio of energy transmitted to incident energy)</p>\r\n\t</li>\r\n\t<li>\r\n\t<p><strong>Opaque Body</strong><br />\r\n\tFor an opaque body: \r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$\\alpha  +  \\rho  = 1 $ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 217</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n\t</li>\r\n\t<li>\r\n\t<p><strong>Gray Body:</strong><br />\r\n\tA gray body is one for which, <span class=\"math display\">\\[\\alpha = \\epsilon, (0 &lt; \\alpha &lt; 1; 0 &lt; \\epsilon &lt; 1),\\]</span> where <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\epsilon\\)</span> = the emissivity of the body For a gray body:</p>\r\n\r\n<div class=\"text-center\">\r\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-light shadow-lg\">$\\epsilon + \\rho = 1$ <span class=\"text-muted\">&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; Page 217</span></button>\r\n</div><br/>\r\n\t</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>Real bodies are frequently approximated as gray bodies.<br />\r\nA <strong>diffused surface</strong> is a surface from which directions of emitted, reflected and incident radiations are unknown or is unpredictable.</p>",
    "important": "General body:\r\n\\[\\alpha + \\rho + \\tau = 1\\]",
    "resources": "<div class=\"mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 flex flex-wrap gap-2\"><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-f0SpdPCoDt-TZoTGQxMF9mGbrl_V-wF/preview\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"px-3 py-1.5 bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-900/40 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 rounded text-xs font-bold hover:bg-blue-200 transition-colors\">📄 Homework</a><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/139QUemJguL77OKTWj_66DD9aMh1mP1yn/preview\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"px-3 py-1.5 bg-green-100 dark:bg-green-900/40 text-green-700 dark:text-green-300 rounded text-xs font-bold hover:bg-green-200 transition-colors\">✅ Solution</a><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/11FoF9hDZJ19SK2une6R_tJeRfgPNrxoZ/preview\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"px-3 py-1.5 bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-900/40 text-purple-700 dark:text-purple-300 rounded text-xs font-bold hover:bg-purple-200 transition-colors\">👨‍🏫 Tutoring</a></div>",
